# file_get_contents not working?

When `file_get_contents` fails on a production server while succeeding on your local XAMPP environment, it rarely indicates a bug in your PHP logic. It almost always points to a difference in the runtime environment, specifically regarding how the server handles network requests and security policies. The code you provided attempts to fetch HTML from an external URL and parse it with `preg_match_all`. While the syntax is valid, the behavior changes drastically depending on whether `allow_url_fopen` is enabled or if cURL is configured to handle remote resources.

In your specific case, the issue isn't necessarily that `file_get_contents` "doesn't work," but rather that it might be returning an empty string (`""`) or a truncated response on the server, causing your regex pattern to fail or match unintended content. The discrepancy between localhost and the server usually stems from one of three factors: disabled protocol wrappers, firewall restrictions, or missing SSL certificates for HTTPS endpoints.

### The `allow_url_fopen` Configuration

You mentioned that `allow_url_fopen = on` in your configuration. This directive is critical when using `file_get_contents` to access remote URLs. On many shared hosting environments or hardened production servers (like those running Nginx or Apache with specific security modules), this setting is often disabled by default or restricted to file paths only.

If `allow_url_fopen` is off, PHP will throw a warning and return false for the URL request. However, if you see different output rather than an error, it suggests the data being received is incomplete. The first regex pattern you attempted includes `<tr>` tags:
```php
$xhtml='|<tr style="background-color:#dddddd;">
        <td class="odd" align="left">(.+?)</td><td class="odd">(.+?)</td>
    </tr>|i';
```
This pattern expects a full table row context. If the server response is truncated or if the HTML encoding differs slightly (e.g., uppercase `<TR>` vs lowercase `<tr>`), this strict pattern will fail to match, resulting in an empty `$score` array after `array_shift`.

The second pattern you found that works:
```php
$xhtml='|<td class="odd" align="left">(.+?)</td><td class="odd">(.+?)</td>|i';
```
works because it is less dependent on the surrounding `<tr>` structure. If the server returns the content but without the expected row wrappers due to parsing issues, this looser pattern still captures the cell data. This highlights a classic debugging strategy: simplify the regex until the target data appears, then reconstruct the pattern with stricter boundaries once you confirm the source data is consistent.

### Switching to cURL for Robustness

Relying on `file_get_contents` for scraping external HTML is fragile because it shares the same underlying stream wrappers as file operations. If your server blocks HTTP/HTTPS streams (common in strict security configurations), `file_get_contents` will fail silently or return false.

A more robust approach, especially when dealing with headers, cookies, or specific user agents, is to use cURL. This method bypasses the `allow_url_fopen` limitation entirely and gives you granular control over the request. Here is how you can refactor your code to ensure it works consistently across environments:

```php
$url = 'http://www.site.com/';
$ch = curl_init();

// Set a timeout to prevent hanging requests indefinitely
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_TIMEOUT, 10); 
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, true); // Ensures the response is returned as a string

// Optional: Follow redirects if the target URL uses them
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_FOLLOWLOCATION, true);

$response = curl_exec($ch);
$curl_error = curl_error($ch);

if ($curl_error) {
    echo "cURL error: " . $curl_error;
} else {
    // Now apply your regex logic to the $response variable
    $xhtml = '|<td class="odd" align="left">(.*?)</td><td class="odd">(.*?)</td>|i';

    preg_match_all($xhtml, $response, $score);
    array_shift($score); // Remove the full matches array to keep only groups

    echo "<pre>";
    print_r($score);
    echo "</pre>";
}

curl_close($ch);
```

### Why the Patterns Differed

The reason your first pattern failed on the server likely relates to how the HTML is rendered. If the server renders `<tr>` tags dynamically or if there are encoding differences (UTF-8 vs ASCII), a strict regex including the row tag will break. By removing the `<tr>` constraint, you allowed the regex to match the specific cell data regardless of the surrounding structure.

When debugging cross-environment issues, always check the raw output of the HTTP request. On your localhost, you might see the full 50KB page, while on the server, a proxy or WAF (Web Application Firewall) might be stripping parts of the response or blocking the specific user agent string sent by `file_get_contents`.

If you are building this within a Laravel application, consider using the native HTTP client provided by the framework's service container. It offers a more modern interface and better error reporting than raw PHP functions:

```php
$response = Http::get('http://www.site.com/');

if ($response->successful()) {
    $html = $response->body();
    // Proceed with regex logic here
}
```

This approach ensures you are explicitly handling the response status and avoids potential stream wrapper issues entirely.
